# Caddy reverse-proxy + TLS terminator for the staging API.
#
# Lives on the DigitalOcean droplet at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.
# This file is the source of truth — when you change something, update
# the droplet copy via the procedure in ./README.md.
#
# Architecture:
#   public internet  ─TLS─►  Caddy (host-level, port 443)
#                            ├─ Let's Encrypt cert auto-renewal
#                            ├─ gzip + zstd response compression
#                            └─ reverse_proxy → 127.0.0.1:3001
#                                              │
#                                              └─ chopnow-staging-api (Docker)
#                                                  └─ port 3001 bound to 127.0.0.1 only
#                                                     (compose: deploy/docker-compose.staging.yml)
#
# Why Caddy is NOT in docker-compose.staging.yml:
#   Caddy needs to bind ports 80 + 443 on the host AND manage cert
#   storage in /var/lib/caddy. Running it in compose would force
#   either port-mapping ceremony, ACME cert hand-off, or a shared
#   volume mount — all of which trade off the simplicity that makes
#   Caddy worth using in the first place. Installing it natively
#   (`apt install caddy`) and pointing it at the loopback API
#   container is the canonical pattern for a single-droplet deploy.

{
    email kanmegnea@gmail.com
}

api-staging.tchopnow.app {
    encode gzip zstd
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
    header {
        -Server
        Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
    }
}
